WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:01.600
Howard A. Rodman: Hi, I’m Howard Rodman.

00:00:01.600 --> 00:00:07.879
I’m president of the Writers Guild West
and also Chair of the 2017 USC Scripter Selection

00:00:07.879 --> 00:00:08.879
Committee.

00:00:08.879 --> 00:00:12.860
Film Courage: We’re curious what you would
recommend to beginning writers on trying to

00:00:12.860 --> 00:00:15.880
present their first screenplay or protect
their work.

00:00:15.880 --> 00:00:17.080
What are some of the few things you recommend?

00:00:17.080 --> 00:00:21.330
Howard A. Rodman: First thing I recommend
to beginning writers is that the only way

00:00:21.330 --> 00:00:28.250
that I absolutely guarantee that you will
not have a career as a writer is to quit.

00:00:28.250 --> 00:00:32.609
So what I recommend to beginning writers is,
real simple, real basic, is that you write

00:00:32.609 --> 00:00:37.649
every day in terms of getting our work out
there in the world.

00:00:37.649 --> 00:00:42.420
If you’re shy or a shoegazer, you have to
be aggressive on behalf of your work and get

00:00:42.420 --> 00:00:47.409
it into the hands of anyone who might conceivably
read it, as painful and humiliating as that

00:00:47.409 --> 00:00:48.739
process is.

00:00:48.739 --> 00:00:52.329
In terms of protecting your work you should
absolutely copyright it.

00:00:52.329 --> 00:00:53.489
Put the C in the circle.

00:00:53.489 --> 00:00:55.289
Get the form from the U.S. Copyright Office.

00:00:55.289 --> 00:00:56.699
The forms are online.

00:00:56.699 --> 00:00:57.760
It’s really simple.

00:00:57.760 --> 00:01:01.109
And also register with the Writer’s Guild.

00:01:01.109 --> 00:01:06.080
The two give very complimentary protections
for work for new people.

00:01:06.080 --> 00:01:09.399
Film Courage: You are a writer, as well?

00:01:09.399 --> 00:01:10.610
You’ve written screenplays?

00:01:10.610 --> 00:01:14.930
Can you share with us the first film that
inspired you to want to write?

00:01:14.930 --> 00:01:16.759
Howard A. Rodman: I’m a writer.

00:01:16.759 --> 00:01:18.729
See the calluses [holds up his hands to the
camera].

00:01:18.729 --> 00:01:24.621
And there are so many films that inspired
me to want to write that to pick one I think

00:01:24.621 --> 00:01:25.621
would be crazy.

00:01:25.621 --> 00:01:34.439
But I think I remember there is a scene at
the opening of an old Film Noir boxing movie

00:01:34.439 --> 00:01:40.860
called BODY AND SOUL, I believe written by
Abraham Lincoln Polonsky, Directed by Robert

00:01:40.860 --> 00:01:42.320
Rossen.

00:01:42.320 --> 00:01:47.789
And there is a boxer, a very proud, sort of
first generation, scrappy immigrant boxer

00:01:47.789 --> 00:01:52.969
played by John Garfield and the mob boss is
offering him a lot of money not to win the

00:01:52.969 --> 00:01:53.969
fight.

00:01:53.969 --> 00:01:56.479
All the money has been bet the other way.

00:01:56.479 --> 00:02:00.859
And John Garfield starts talking about his
pride and his morals and the ethics of throwing

00:02:00.859 --> 00:02:06.229
a fight and the mob boss turns to him and
says “There is addition.

00:02:06.229 --> 00:02:07.429
There is subtraction.

00:02:07.429 --> 00:02:09.830
The rest is conversation.”

00:02:09.830 --> 00:02:10.830
And I just thought “Wow!

00:02:10.830 --> 00:02:13.740
If I could just like write one of those!”

00:02:13.740 --> 00:02:19.670
And I think for many of us we start reading
books and watching movies because somebody

00:02:19.670 --> 00:02:24.520
has built a world and we can just hide in
that world for awhile and then you realize.

00:02:24.520 --> 00:02:25.520
“Oh, wait!

00:02:25.520 --> 00:02:29.770
I could build me own sort of purpose-built
world that would really fit me.”

00:02:29.770 --> 00:02:34.550
And so the transition from movie going to
reading to writing I think for many of us

00:02:34.550 --> 00:02:37.750
is just a slow descent into the pit.

00:02:37.750 --> 00:02:42.980
Film Courage: So the first screenplay that
you wrote did you have the similar sort of

00:02:42.980 --> 00:02:46.590
dream that this was going to be the first
ONE and it would put you on the map Like so

00:02:46.590 --> 00:02:50.610
many new writers think that the first screenplay
will open doors.

00:02:50.610 --> 00:02:52.140
Actually even the first draft [of a first
screenplay].

00:02:52.140 --> 00:02:54.390
Howard A. Rodman: I never had any thoughts
like that.

00:02:54.390 --> 00:03:02.950
I spent well over a decade writing unpublishable
novels before I transitioned to writing unproduceable

00:03:02.950 --> 00:03:03.950
screenplays.

00:03:03.950 --> 00:03:04.950
So I was under no illusion.

00:03:04.950 --> 00:03:09.640
I just started having ideas that were movies
that weren’t quite books.

00:03:09.640 --> 00:03:15.171
And the first of those screenplays that I
wrote still has not been produced but I’ve

00:03:15.171 --> 00:03:18.230
gotten tons and tons of work off of it.

00:03:18.230 --> 00:03:23.320
It’s like of course “We can’t do this
because it’s too weird, BUT maybe you can

00:03:23.320 --> 00:03:24.320
do that?”

00:03:24.320 --> 00:03:30.010
And so I don’t think I ever really expected
frankly that anything I wrote would ever be

00:03:30.010 --> 00:03:31.010
filmed.

00:03:31.010 --> 00:03:35.160
And when things I write do get filmed, it
just strikes me as sort of the bonus rounds

00:03:35.160 --> 00:03:36.310
in a pinball game.

00:03:36.310 --> 00:03:39.060
It’s sort of unexpected and really lovely.

00:03:39.060 --> 00:03:41.180
Film Courage: Wrapping up here.

00:03:41.180 --> 00:03:42.320
I know you have to go.

00:03:42.320 --> 00:03:46.490
Wondering what you see as the most common
mistakes or somethings new writers should

00:03:46.490 --> 00:03:50.950
be weary of regarding formatting, structure,
things like that?

00:03:50.950 --> 00:03:55.100
Howard A. Rodman: I would tell beginning writers
not to worry too much about about formatting

00:03:55.100 --> 00:03:56.630
and structure.

00:03:56.630 --> 00:04:03.041
In terms of formatting there are many really
good software programs and some of them you

00:04:03.041 --> 00:04:06.600
can get for free and so let them worry about
the formatting.

00:04:06.600 --> 00:04:10.820
In terms of the structure, I think you can’t
say “My God!

00:04:10.820 --> 00:04:13.760
My first act didn’t end on page 29.

00:04:13.760 --> 00:04:21.070
I’m a bad writer” if you find real living
characters through telling a story that only

00:04:21.070 --> 00:04:26.000
you can tell or write about a place in the
world that you know better than anybody else,

00:04:26.000 --> 00:04:30.850
just let the story tell itself, or let the
characters tell the story and the rest will

00:04:30.850 --> 00:04:31.910
fall into place.

00:04:31.910 --> 00:04:36.610
Film Courage: So in other words, for lack
of a better term, get the vomit draft, please

00:04:36.610 --> 00:04:42.000
excuse my crass analogy, but get that out
and worry about all the other things later?

00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:43.790
Howard A. Rodman: No, I wouldn’t even say
that.

00:04:43.790 --> 00:04:48.670
I would say…I was the editor-in-chief of
my college newspaper.

00:04:48.670 --> 00:04:53.590
And there is a big sign in the newspaper office
underneath this huge clock, that had a blood

00:04:53.590 --> 00:04:57.660
red second hand and the sign said “This
is a daily, not a weekly.”

00:04:57.660 --> 00:05:02.350
And whenever we were there past midnight the
managing editor would put up another sign

00:05:02.350 --> 00:05:05.139
that would say “Don’t get it right, get
it written.”

00:05:05.139 --> 00:05:09.280
And I would say the same thing to anyone starting
out writing and to people who have been writing

00:05:09.280 --> 00:05:11.100
all of their lives.

00:05:11.100 --> 00:05:12.100
Film Courage: I love it!

00:05:12.100 --> 00:05:12.130
Thank you!

